## App-owned decisions to complete

- Product flow and acceptance: describe the real frontend interaction, server request, processing/data behavior and visible response; distinguish local and deployed proof.
- Architecture: choose runtime/framework, frontend/server boundaries, API request/response/error schemas and compatibility. Add persistence only when required, with migration/restore behavior if used.
- Security/configuration: document server-only secret names, public configuration, required auth/authorization and validation boundaries. Actual values and provider targets stay private.
- Commands: document actual install, development, tests, typecheck, build and browser verification commands; implement them with pinned versions/lockfiles and app-owned CI. This seed does not implement commands or CI.
- Deploy/resume: document agreed targets, reviewed deployment/protection/bootstrap path, compatible frontend/backend source versions, health checks, rollback, private inventory/recovery references and how a fresh task clones and resumes this repo.

## Shared full-stack rules

A full-stack app includes real server execution used by the frontend. Validate requests on the server, constrain errors, and keep privileged data/credentials out of browser bundles. Authorization belongs at the server boundary when required. Test successful and invalid requests plus the actual browser → API → data/response path; verify persistence across requests/restarts only when persistence is part of the requirements.

Use the smallest suitable supported architecture, including framework-native server endpoints when appropriate. AWS is a deployment choice requiring a project-specific reviewed role/IaC profile. Record actual app checks, CI status, source/deployment identity and deployed behavior; tool installation, local mocks and provider identity reads do not prove the flow works.

